---
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro'
import Preview from '~/components/Preview.astro'
import PropsTable from '~/components/PropsTable.astro'
import OverlaysDemo from '~/demos/overlays-demo'
import CommandMenuDemo from '~/demos/command-menu-demo'
---

<DocsShell
  title="Overlays"
  description="Dialogs, drawers, menus, popovers and the command palette."
>
  <p>
    These are built on Base UI, which supplies focus trapping, focus restoration, Escape handling,
    scroll locking and the ARIA wiring. Reimplementing that correctly is a project in itself, and
    getting it wrong is invisible until someone tries to use the app without a mouse.
  </p>

  <Preview demo="overlays-demo" title="Every overlay" contained={false}>
    <OverlaysDemo client:visible />
  </Preview>

  <h2 id="dialog">Dialog and AlertDialog</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>Dialog</code> can be dismissed by clicking outside or pressing Escape. An
    <code>AlertDialog</code> ignores an outside press, for decisions where an accidental dismissal would
    lose work — but it still closes on Escape, because a modal that traps focus and cannot be escaped
    is an accessibility failure. Treat that dismissal as a cancel.
  </p>
  <PropsTable type="DialogContentProps" element="div" />

  <h2 id="command-menu">CommandMenu</h2>
  <p>
    A keyboard-first palette. Open state belongs to the application, so a hotkey, a menu item and a
    button can all drive it. Matching includes each action's <code>keywords</code>, so "ship" can
    find "Deploy latest build".
  </p>
  <Preview
    demo="command-menu-demo"
    title="Command palette"
    description="Press Command-K or Control-K anywhere on this page."
  >
    <CommandMenuDemo client:visible />
  </Preview>
  <PropsTable type="CommandMenuProps" />

  <h3>Accessibility notes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Tab is trapped inside an open modal, and focus returns to the trigger on close.</li>
    <li>The page behind a modal is removed from the accessibility tree.</li>
    <li>
      Tooltips label; they never hold instructions or interactive content, and they are not
      announced on touch. An icon button still needs its own <code>label</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsShell>
